A command has exactly one handler. It is an ordinary class — no base class, no registration —
and the generator finds it through the contract it implements.

## With a result

```csharp
using Stella.Ergosfare.Commands.Abstractions;
using Stella.Ergosfare.Core.Abstractions;

public sealed class PlaceOrderHandler(IOrderRepository repository)
    : ICommandHandler<PlaceOrder, Guid>
{
    public async ValueTask<Guid> HandleAsync(PlaceOrder command, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        var order = Order.Create(command.CustomerId, command.Lines);
        await repository.AddAsync(order, context.CancellationToken);
        return order.Id;
    }
}
```

## With no result

```csharp
public sealed class ArchiveOrderHandler(IOrderRepository repository)
    : ICommandHandler<ArchiveOrder>
{
    public async ValueTask HandleAsync(ArchiveOrder command, ErgosfareContext context)
        => await repository.ArchiveAsync(command.Id, context.CancellationToken);
}
```

## Dependencies

Constructor parameters are resolved from the **dispatching scope**, so a scoped
`IOrderRepository` behaves exactly as it does in a controller. Nothing special is needed:

```csharp
public sealed class PlaceOrderHandler(
    IOrderRepository repository,
    IPricingService pricing,
    ILogger<PlaceOrderHandler> logger) : ICommandHandler<PlaceOrder, Guid> { … }
```

{% aside type="tip" %}
A handler with a single public constructor and plain service parameters can be constructed
directly by a compiled plan, skipping the container entirely. Several public constructors keep
it on the container path and are reported as `ERGO003` — informational, since everything still
works.
{% /aside %}

## Always pass the cancellation token

```csharp
await repository.AddAsync(order, context.CancellationToken);
```

This is the most common omission, and it only shows up under load.

## Dispatching

```csharp
public sealed class OrdersController(ICommandMediator mediator)
{
    public async Task<IResult> Post(PlaceOrder command, CancellationToken ct)
    {
        var id = await mediator.SendAsync(command, ct);
        return Results.Created($"/orders/{id}", id);
    }
}
```

The overloads:

```csharp
await mediator.SendAsync(command);                              // no result
await mediator.SendAsync(command, ct);
var id = await mediator.SendAsync(new PlaceOrder(…));           // with result
var id = await mediator.SendAsync(command, GroupSet.Of("audit"), ct);
await mediator.SendAsync(command, context);                     // share a context
```

When both the message and result types are known at the call site, naming them lets the
dispatch build its executor from arguments the compiler already resolved rather than
constructing it reflectively:

```csharp
var id = await mediator.SendAsync<PlaceOrder, Guid>(command, ct);
```

That matters for NativeAOT rather than for throughput — the two paths measure within a
nanosecond of each other.

## Covariant handlers

A handler registered against a base type serves every command in that family:

```csharp
public sealed class OrderCommandHandler : ICommandHandler<OrderCommand> { … }
```

A **direct** handler always wins over a covariant one, with no ambiguity — that is the idiom for
a default with specific overrides:

```csharp
public sealed class PlaceOrderHandler : ICommandHandler<PlaceOrder> { … }   // wins for PlaceOrder
```

Two handlers at the same level is `ERGO010`, a build error, because the ladder has no
tiebreaker within a level.

## Handlers are not where cross-cutting work goes

Logging, validation, authorization and metrics belong in interceptors — otherwise you write
them again in the next handler. See
[Interceptor or handler](/ergosfare.docs/preview/best-practices/interceptor-or-handler).

## Testing one

Construct it, because it is an ordinary class:

```csharp
var handler = new PlaceOrderHandler(new InMemoryOrderRepository());
var id = await handler.HandleAsync(new PlaceOrder(customerId, lines), new ErgosfareContext());
```

That tests the logic. To test that the handler is *reachable* — that its interceptors run and
the dispatch resolves — build a container and dispatch. See
[Composition root and testing](/ergosfare.docs/preview/best-practices/composition-root).

An event handler — a subscriber — reacts to something that already happened. Every matching one
runs, and none of them answers the publisher.

## Writing one

```csharp
using Stella.Ergosfare.Events.Abstractions;
using Stella.Ergosfare.Core.Abstractions;

public sealed class SendShipmentEmail(IEmailSender sender) : IEventHandler<OrderShipped>
{
    public ValueTask HandleAsync(OrderShipped @event, ErgosfareContext context)
        => sender.SendAsync(@event.CustomerEmail, "Your order shipped", context.CancellationToken);
}
```

`IEventHandler<TEvent>` returns `ValueTask` and nothing else — a broadcast has no result to
produce.

## Many subscribers, one event

```csharp
public sealed class SendShipmentEmail  : IEventHandler<OrderShipped> { … }
public sealed class UpdateReadModel    : IEventHandler<OrderShipped> { … }
public sealed class AuditOrderEvents   : IEventHandler<OrderEvent>   { … }
```

Publishing an `OrderShipped` runs all three. Unlike commands, this is not a contest — `ERGO010`
does not apply to events, because delivering to everyone is the point.

They run in `[Weight]` order, direct registrations before covariant ones. Ordering exists, but
relying on it between subscribers is a smell: if B must run after A, they are probably one
operation.

## When one throws

The exception propagates out of the publish. Subscribers that had already run stay run;
subscribers after it do not.

That is rarely what you want, so a subscriber that can fail should contain its own failure:

```csharp
public sealed class SendShipmentEmail(IEmailSender sender, ILogger<SendShipmentEmail> logger)
    : IEventHandler<OrderShipped>
{
    public async ValueTask HandleAsync(OrderShipped @event, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        try
        {
            await sender.SendAsync(@event.CustomerEmail, "Your order shipped", context.CancellationToken);
        }
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            logger.LogError(ex, "shipment email failed for {Order}", @event.OrderId);
        }
    }
}
```

An exception interceptor on the event pipeline can do this centrally instead. Either way, decide
deliberately — the default is that one failing subscriber stops the rest.

## Publishing

```csharp
await mediator.PublishAsync(new OrderShipped(id, email, at));
await mediator.PublishAsync(@event, ct);
await mediator.PublishAsync(@event, GroupSet.Of("audit"), ct);
await mediator.PublishAsync(@event, context);          // share the dispatching context
```

A publish returns when every subscriber has completed. It is not fire-and-forget: if you want
that, put the work on a queue and let a consumer dispatch it.

{% aside type="note" %}
Every publish requires at least one selected main handler. When the compiler can prove
otherwise, `ERGO005` or `ERGO024` fails the build. Dynamic group sets selecting no handler
throw `NoHandlerFoundException` before interceptors run.
{% /aside %}

## Publishing from a handler

Common, and the shape worth getting right:

```csharp
public sealed class PlaceOrderHandler(IOrderRepository repository, IEventMediator events)
    : ICommandHandler<PlaceOrder, Guid>
{
    public async ValueTask<Guid> HandleAsync(PlaceOrder command, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        var order = Order.Create(command.CustomerId, command.Lines);
        await repository.AddAsync(order, context.CancellationToken);

        await events.PublishAsync(new OrderPlaced(order.Id), context);
        return order.Id;
    }
}
```

Passing `context` keeps the publish in the same logical operation — same items, same
cancellation.

**Watch the transaction boundary.** Publishing before a commit lets a subscriber act on state
that later rolls back. Collect the events and publish after committing — see the
[Unit of work recipe](/ergosfare.docs/preview/recipes/unit-of-work).

## Subscribers get the full pipeline

Interceptors apply to events like anything else: `IEventPreInterceptor`,
`IEventPostInterceptor`, `IEventExceptionInterceptor`, `IEventFinalInterceptor`.

The pre and post seams name the boundary around a *delivery*, so in a broadcast they run once
per subscriber rather than once per publish.

## Registering

Nothing to write — `AddGenerated()` finds subscribers like any other participant:

```csharp
services.AddErgosfare(registry => registry
    .AddEventModule(module => module.AddGenerated()));
```

## Testing

```csharp
var sender = new RecordingEmailSender();
var handler = new SendShipmentEmail(sender);

await handler.HandleAsync(new OrderShipped(id, "a@b.c", DateTimeOffset.UtcNow),
                          new ErgosfareContext());

Assert.Single(sender.Sent);
```

To test that *all* the subscribers you expect run, build a container and publish — that is the
part a unit test cannot tell you.

A query has exactly one handler, found through the contract it implements.

## A single result

```csharp
using Stella.Ergosfare.Queries.Abstractions;
using Stella.Ergosfare.Core.Abstractions;

public sealed class GetProductHandler(IProductRepository repository)
    : IQueryHandler<GetProduct, ProductDto>
{
    public async ValueTask<ProductDto> HandleAsync(GetProduct query, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        var product = await repository.FindAsync(query.Id, context.CancellationToken);
        return ProductDto.From(product);
    }
}
```

## A stream

The method is `StreamAsync`, and it takes the query and the context:

```csharp
public sealed class SearchProductsHandler(IProductRepository repository)
    : IStreamQueryHandler<SearchProducts, ProductDto>
{
    public async IAsyncEnumerable<ProductDto> StreamAsync(
        SearchProducts query, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        await foreach (var product in repository.SearchAsync(query.Term, context.CancellationToken))
            yield return ProductDto.From(product);
    }
}
```

Cancellation comes from `context.CancellationToken`, so there is no separate token parameter to
forget — pass it into whatever you enumerate.

{% aside type="note" %}
A `null` element is a legitimate element. If your sequence is `IAsyncEnumerable<ProductDto?>`
and you yield `null`, the caller receives it — the pipeline does not filter nulls out.
{% /aside %}

## Dispatching

```csharp
var product = await mediator.QueryAsync(new GetProduct(id));
var product = await mediator.QueryAsync(new GetProduct(id), ct);
var product = await mediator.QueryAsync(new GetProduct(id), GroupSet.Of("cached"), ct);
```

And a stream:

```csharp
await foreach (var product in mediator.StreamAsync(new SearchProducts("bench"))
                                      .WithCancellation(ct))
{
    …
}
```

When both types are known at the call site, naming them keeps the dispatch from constructing its
executor reflectively — which matters for NativeAOT rather than throughput:

```csharp
var product = await mediator.QueryAsync<GetProduct, ProductDto>(new GetProduct(id), ct);
```

The streaming members stay untyped for now: their shape is under revision, and adding a surface
to something scheduled to change is work that has to be undone.

## Keep queries read-only

Nothing enforces it, and that is the point of having two modules — a query that writes is a
command that lied about itself. It also breaks reasonable assumptions: a caching interceptor on
the query pipeline is safe precisely because queries do not have effects.

## Dependencies

Constructor parameters come from the dispatching scope, like any other service. A read-only
`DbContext`, a Dapper connection factory, a cache — all ordinary registrations.

## Covariant handlers

A handler on a base query type serves the family, and a direct handler wins outright:

```csharp
public sealed class TenantQueryHandler<TResult> : IQueryHandler<TenantQuery<TResult>, TResult> { … }
```

Two handlers at the same level is `ERGO010`.

## Testing

```csharp
var handler = new GetProductHandler(new InMemoryProductRepository(seed));
var dto = await handler.HandleAsync(new GetProduct(id), new ErgosfareContext());
```

For a stream, drain it:

```csharp
var results = new List<ProductDto>();
await foreach (var item in handler.StreamAsync(query, new ErgosfareContext()))
    results.Add(item);
```

Every handler and interceptor receives an `ErgosfareContext`. It carries the cancellation token,
a bag of per-dispatch state, and the way to stop a pipeline.

```csharp
public ValueTask<Guid> HandleAsync(CreateProduct command, ErgosfareContext context)
{
    context.CancellationToken.ThrowIfCancellationRequested();
    …
}
```

## Cancellation

`context.CancellationToken` is the token the dispatch was started with. Pass it down rather
than reaching for `CancellationToken.None`:

```csharp
await repository.AddAsync(product, context.CancellationToken);
```

Every mediator call takes a token, and the ones that do not take one explicitly default it:

```csharp
await mediator.SendAsync(command, cancellationToken);
```

## Per-dispatch state

`Items` is a dictionary shared by every participant in one dispatch. It is how a pre-interceptor
tells a later stage something without changing the message:

```csharp
public sealed class StartTimer : ICommandPreInterceptor
{
    public ValueTask<object> HandleAsync(ICommand command, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        context.Set("startedAt", Stopwatch.GetTimestamp());
        return ValueTask.FromResult<object>(command);
    }
}

public sealed class RecordDuration : ICommandFinalInterceptor
{
    public ValueTask HandleAsync(
        IMessage message, object? result, Exception? exception, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        if (context.TryGet<long>("startedAt", out var started))
            _metrics.Record(Stopwatch.GetElapsedTime(started));

        return ValueTask.CompletedTask;
    }
}
```

The accessors:

| Member | What it does |
|---|---|
| `Set(key, value)` | Stores a value. |
| `Has(key)` | Whether a key is present. |
| `Get<T>(key)` | Reads it, throwing when absent or the wrong type. |
| `TryGet<T>(key, out value)` | Reads it without throwing. |
| `Items` | The underlying dictionary, for anything the above does not cover. |

{% aside type="caution" %}
`Items` is per dispatch, not per request. A nested dispatch gets its own context unless you
pass yours explicitly, so it is the wrong place for something an HTTP request scope should own.
Put that in a scoped service and inject it.
{% /aside %}

## Stopping the pipeline

`Abort()` ends the dispatch immediately:

```csharp
public sealed class RequireTenant : ICommandPreInterceptor
{
    public ValueTask<object> HandleAsync(ICommand command, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        if (_tenant.Current is null)
            context.Abort("no tenant on this request");

        return ValueTask.FromResult<object>(command);
    }
}
```

It throws `ExecutionAbortedException`, and the pipeline treats that as a stop rather than a
failure: **the exception stage does not run, and neither does the final stage.** The signal
travels to the caller unchanged.

That distinction is the whole point of having a separate signal. A failure is something that
went wrong and that a final interceptor should still observe; an abort is a decision that this
dispatch should not continue.

Overloads carry a reason and a value:

```csharp
context.Abort("not authorized");
context.Abort("quota exceeded", remainingQuota);
```

## Passing a context along

Every dispatch surface takes an optional context. Passing yours makes the nested dispatch share
your `Items` and your cancellation:

```csharp
public async ValueTask HandleAsync(PlaceOrder command, ErgosfareContext context)
{
    await _events.PublishAsync(new OrderPlaced(command.Id), context);
}
```

Omitting it gives the nested dispatch a fresh context instead. Which you want depends on whether
the inner work is part of the same logical operation — see
[Nested dispatch](/ergosfare.docs/preview/core-concepts/nested-dispatch).

## Scopes

`CreateScope()` gives a nested lifetime within one dispatch, for state that should not outlive
a section of the pipeline:

```csharp
using var scope = context.CreateScope();
```

## Why it is pooled

Contexts are pooled and returned when the dispatch completes. That is what keeps a dispatch from
allocating one per call on the hot path, and it has one consequence worth knowing:

{% aside type="danger" %}
**Do not keep a context after your participant returns.** Storing it in a field, closing over it
in a background task, or handing it to something that outlives the dispatch means you are
holding an object that has already been returned to the pool and may now belong to an unrelated
dispatch. Copy the values you need instead.
{% /aside %}

```csharp
// wrong — the context is gone by the time this runs
_ = Task.Run(() => Audit(context.Get<string>("user")));

// right — read it first
var user = context.Get<string>("user");
_ = Task.Run(() => Audit(user));
```

The pooling is also why there is no `AsyncLocal` anywhere in the dispatch path: the context is a
parameter, so it flows exactly as far as you pass it and no further.

Interceptors are how cross-cutting work attaches to a dispatch without the handler knowing
about it. They are discovered and registered exactly like handlers, and they run as part of the
same compiled pipeline.

## The four stages

```
pre*  →  main handler  →  post*        →  final*
                       ↘  exception*  ↗
```

- **pre** runs before the handler, and may rewrite the message.
- **post** runs after a successful handler, and may rewrite the result.
- **exception** runs when the handler or an interceptor threw, and may supply a result instead.
- **final** always runs last, on success and on failure alike.

Within a stage, order is `[Weight]` descending, with direct registrations before covariant ones.
See [Groups and weights](/ergosfare.docs/preview/cross-cutting-concerns/groups-and-weights).

## Pre-interceptors

A pre-interceptor returns the message the next stage will see. Returning the input unchanged is
the pass-through case:

```csharp
public sealed class LogCommands(ILogger<LogCommands> logger) : ICommandPreInterceptor
{
    public ValueTask<object> HandleAsync(ICommand command, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        logger.LogInformation("Dispatching {Command}", command.GetType().Name);
        return ValueTask.FromResult<object>(command);
    }
}
```

Two arities are available, trading breadth for type safety:

| Interface | `HandleAsync` signature | Use when |
|---|---|---|
| `ICommandPreInterceptor` | `ValueTask<object>(ICommand, ErgosfareContext)` | it applies to every command |
| `ICommandPreInterceptor<TCommand>` | `ValueTask<TCommand>(TCommand, ErgosfareContext)` | it applies to one command or hierarchy |

The typed form returns the typed message, so rewriting needs no cast:

```csharp
public sealed class NormalizeName : ICommandPreInterceptor<CreateProduct>
{
    public ValueTask<CreateProduct> HandleAsync(CreateProduct command, ErgosfareContext context)
        => ValueTask.FromResult(command with { Name = command.Name.Trim() });
}
```

Returning a *derived* message is allowed and often the point — `TCommand` is the contract, and
anything assignable to it satisfies that:

```csharp
public sealed class Enrich : ICommandPreInterceptor<PlaceOrder>
{
    public ValueTask<PlaceOrder> HandleAsync(PlaceOrder command, ErgosfareContext context)
        => ValueTask.FromResult<PlaceOrder>(new ValidatedPlaceOrder(command));
}
```

{% aside type="note" %}
Earlier previews had a two-parameter form, `ICommandPreInterceptor<TCommand, TModified>`. It
was removed: since the one-parameter form started returning the typed message, the second
argument bought nothing. Migration is a deletion — drop the second type argument and the
interceptor keeps returning what it returned.
{% /aside %}

## Post-interceptors

A post-interceptor sees a successful result and may replace it:

```csharp
public sealed class StampCreatedAt : ICommandPostInterceptor<CreateProduct, Guid>
{
    public ValueTask<Guid> HandleAsync(CreateProduct command, Guid result, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        _metrics.Record(command.Name);
        return ValueTask.FromResult(result);
    }
}
```

Three arities:

| Interface | Signature |
|---|---|
| `ICommandPostInterceptor` | `ValueTask<object>(ICommand, object, ErgosfareContext)` |
| `ICommandPostInterceptor<TCommand>` | `ValueTask<object>(TCommand, object, ErgosfareContext)` |
| `ICommandPostInterceptor<TCommand, TResult>` | `ValueTask<TResult>(TCommand, TResult, ErgosfareContext)` |

For a void pipeline the result slot carries `Unit`, so a post-interceptor written against a
command with no result still has something well-defined to receive.

## Exception interceptors

An exception interceptor runs when something threw, and it can answer the dispatch with a result
instead of letting the exception escape:

```csharp
public sealed class TranslateNotFound
    : ICommandExceptionInterceptor<GetProduct, ProductDto>
{
    public ValueTask<ProductDto> HandleAsync(
        GetProduct command, ProductDto? result, Exception exception, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        if (exception is NotFoundException)
            return ValueTask.FromResult(ProductDto.Empty);

        throw exception;
    }
}
```

Note the asymmetry, which is deliberate: **the result comes in nullable and goes out
non-nullable.** The handler may have thrown before producing anything, so there genuinely may
be no result yet — but a dispatch locked its result type at the call site, and no stage
downstream may hand the caller a `null` it never asked for.

An interceptor that cannot produce a result should not claim the failure. Rethrow, or do not
match; an unmatched stage lets the exception surface. Code that wants to model absence models
it in the value, which is what a result carrier is for.

Once any exception interceptor has run, the exception is considered handled and the pipeline
continues to the final stage. If none matched, it surfaces.

### Filtering by exception type

Implementing the `For` variant narrows an interceptor to one exception type, which reads better
than an `is` check and lets the compiled plan bake the test:

```csharp
public sealed class HandleTimeouts
    : ICommandExceptionInterceptorFor<PlaceOrder, Guid, TimeoutException>
{
    public ValueTask<Guid> HandleAsync(
        PlaceOrder command, Guid? result, TimeoutException exception, ErgosfareContext context)
        => ValueTask.FromResult(Guid.Empty);
}
```

## Final interceptors

A final interceptor always runs — after success, after a handled failure, after an unhandled
one. It returns nothing, because by then the dispatch's answer is settled:

```csharp
public sealed class RecordDuration : ICommandFinalInterceptor<PlaceOrder, Guid>
{
    public ValueTask HandleAsync(
        PlaceOrder command, Guid? result, Exception? exception, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        _metrics.Record(command.GetType().Name, exception is null);
        return ValueTask.CompletedTask;
    }
}
```

Both the result and the exception are nullable here, and for the same reason: it runs from a
`finally`, so either may be absent.

The one case where it does *not* run is a deliberate stop — see below.

## Stopping a pipeline

Any participant can end the dispatch early by throwing `ExecutionAbortedException`:

```csharp
public sealed class RejectUnauthorized : ICommandPreInterceptor
{
    public ValueTask<object> HandleAsync(ICommand command, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        if (!_user.IsAuthenticated)
            throw new ExecutionAbortedException("not authenticated");

        return ValueTask.FromResult<object>(command);
    }
}
```

This is not a failure and is not treated as one: the exception stage does not run, the final
stage does not run, and the signal travels to the caller. It says "this dispatch should not
continue", not "this dispatch went wrong".

## Which module's contracts to use

Each module has its own interceptor contracts — `ICommandPreInterceptor`,
`IQueryPreInterceptor`, `IEventPreInterceptor` — and they carry that module's marker, which is
how discovery finds them.

For something genuinely universal, implement the core contracts in
`Stella.Ergosfare.Core.Abstractions.Handlers` against `IMessage`. That reaches every module's
messages, at the cost of a weaker static type.

## What this costs

Nothing per dispatch that you did not ask for. The stages are assembled at compile time, and
for a pipeline whose shape is fully settled, the generated plan is a straight line of calls
with the interceptors in place — no descriptor list, no ordering work, and for participants
provably constructible, no container resolution either.

A pipeline with no interceptors at all pays for none: it is the same straight line without the
calls.

A final interceptor runs last, whether the dispatch succeeded or failed. It returns nothing,
because by the time it runs the dispatch's answer is already settled.

## The contracts

```csharp
ICommandFinalInterceptor                        // every command
ICommandFinalInterceptor<TCommand>              // one command
ICommandFinalInterceptor<TCommand, TResult>     // typed result
```

Queries have the same three; events have the ones without a result.

## The shape

```csharp
public sealed class RecordOutcome(IMetrics metrics) : ICommandFinalInterceptor
{
    public ValueTask HandleAsync(
        IMessage message, object? result, Exception? exception, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        metrics.Record(message.GetType().Name, succeeded: exception is null);
        return ValueTask.CompletedTask;
    }
}
```

Both the result and the exception are nullable, and both can be `null` at once — a void
pipeline that succeeded has neither a meaningful result nor a failure. Read them as a pair:

| `exception` | `result` | What happened |
|---|---|---|
| `null` | a value | the handler succeeded |
| `null` | `null` | a void pipeline succeeded |
| set | `null` | it failed and nothing answered |
| set | a value | it failed and the exception stage answered |

## The one case where it does not run

`context.Abort()`.

An abort is a deliberate stop rather than a failure, so it skips the exception stage **and this
one**, and travels to the caller unchanged.

{% aside type="caution" %}
This matters when a pre-interceptor opened something that the final stage was going to close.
If any interceptor can abort after that point, the resource leaks.

Two ways out: put the aborting check ahead of whatever opens the resource, with a higher
`[Weight]` — or open it in the handler where a `try`/`finally` covers every exit.
{% /aside %}

## Pairing with a pre-interceptor

The common shape is a pre-interceptor that records something and a final one that reads it:

```csharp
public sealed class StartTimer : ICommandPreInterceptor
{
    public ValueTask<object> HandleAsync(ICommand command, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        context.Set("startedAt", Stopwatch.GetTimestamp());
        return ValueTask.FromResult<object>(command);
    }
}

public sealed class StopTimer(IMetrics metrics) : ICommandFinalInterceptor
{
    public ValueTask HandleAsync(
        IMessage message, object? result, Exception? exception, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        if (context.TryGet<long>("startedAt", out var started))
            metrics.Record(message.GetType().Name, Stopwatch.GetElapsedTime(started));

        return ValueTask.CompletedTask;
    }
}
```

Use `TryGet` rather than `Get`. The final interceptor may run for a dispatch where the pre one
never did — a different group, or an interceptor added later — and it should not throw while
cleaning up.

## Ordering

`[Weight]` descending, direct before covariant. When a final interceptor closes something,
give it a weight that puts it after anything that reads what it closes.

## Do not throw from here

An exception from a final interceptor escapes into whatever the pipeline was already doing. If
the dispatch was failing, you have replaced a meaningful failure with a bookkeeping one.

Wrap anything that can fail:

```csharp
try
{
    await _audit.WriteAsync(row, context.CancellationToken);
}
catch (Exception ex)
{
    _logger.LogWarning(ex, "audit write failed");
}
```

Also be careful with the cancellation token here: if the dispatch failed *because* it was
cancelled, the token is already cancelled and any work you start with it fails immediately.

## Typical uses

**Timing and metrics** for every outcome.

**Audit rows** recording that an operation was attempted, with its result.

**Releasing something** a pre-interceptor took — subject to the abort caveat above.

**Committing or rolling back a transaction**, which is exactly the both-paths shape this stage
exists for. See the [Unit of work recipe](/ergosfare.docs/preview/recipes/unit-of-work).

## What does not belong here

**Changing the answer.** It returns `void`; the answer is settled. Use post or exception.

**Work the caller waits on that is not part of the operation.** A final interceptor is awaited
inside the dispatch, so slow work here slows every call. Queue it instead.

A post-interceptor runs after the handler returned successfully, and may replace the result. It
does not run when the handler threw; that is the exception stage.

## The contracts

```csharp
ICommandPostInterceptor                          // every command, untyped result
ICommandPostInterceptor<TCommand>                // one command, untyped result
ICommandPostInterceptor<TCommand, TResult>       // one command, typed result
```

Queries have the same three. Events have the first two — a broadcast produces no result.

## Observing

```csharp
public sealed class CountSuccesses(IMetrics metrics) : ICommandPostInterceptor
{
    public ValueTask<object> HandleAsync(
        ICommand command, object result, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        metrics.Increment(command.GetType().Name);
        return ValueTask.FromResult(result);
    }
}
```

Return the result you were given. Whatever you return is what the next stage — and eventually
the caller — sees.

## Reshaping

The typed form is the one worth reaching for when you actually touch the result:

```csharp
public sealed class CacheProduct(IMemoryCache cache)
    : IQueryPostInterceptor<GetProduct, ProductDto>
{
    public ValueTask<ProductDto> HandleAsync(
        GetProduct query, ProductDto result, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        cache.Set(query.Id, result, TimeSpan.FromMinutes(5));
        return ValueTask.FromResult(result);
    }
}
```

Replacing the result is the same shape — return something else:

```csharp
public sealed class Redact : IQueryPostInterceptor<GetCustomer, CustomerDto>
{
    public ValueTask<CustomerDto> HandleAsync(
        GetCustomer query, CustomerDto result, ErgosfareContext context)
        => ValueTask.FromResult(result with { Email = "***" });
}
```

## Void pipelines

A command with no result still has a result slot: it carries `Unit`. That is what lets a
post-interceptor written against a void command receive something well-defined:

```csharp
public sealed class LogCompletion : ICommandPostInterceptor<ArchiveOrder>
{
    public ValueTask<object> HandleAsync(
        ArchiveOrder command, object result, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        _logger.LogInformation("Archived {Id}", command.Id);
        return ValueTask.FromResult(result);
    }
}
```

`Unit` is a value, not `null`. Return what you were handed.

## Ordering

`[Weight]` descending, direct before covariant — the same rule as every stage. When results are
being transformed, order matters more than usual, because each interceptor sees what the last
one returned:

```csharp
[Weight(100)] public sealed class Redact  : IQueryPostInterceptor<GetCustomer, CustomerDto> { … }
[Weight(50)]  public sealed class Envelope: IQueryPostInterceptor<GetCustomer, CustomerDto> { … }
```

## Typical uses

**Caching**, populating on the way out while a pre-interceptor checks on the way in.

**Enveloping** a result into a transport shape, when you would otherwise write it in every
handler.

**Metrics and audit** for successful operations specifically — the failure side belongs to the
exception or final stage.

**Appending to an outbox** — though be careful about ordering relative to a transaction commit;
see the [Unit of work recipe](/ergosfare.docs/preview/recipes/unit-of-work).

## What does not belong here

**Cleanup.** A post-interceptor does not run when the handler threw. Anything that must happen
either way goes in a final interceptor.

**Failure translation.** That is the exception stage, which can answer the dispatch with a
result.

**Work that should be part of the result.** If the handler's result is incomplete without this
interceptor, the handler is incomplete. An interceptor that every caller depends on is not
cross-cutting — it is the operation.

A pre-interceptor runs before the handler and returns the message the next stage will see. It
is the stage for anything that decides whether the work should happen, or fixes up its input
first.

## The contracts

```csharp
ICommandPreInterceptor                    // every command
ICommandPreInterceptor<TCommand>          // one command, or one hierarchy
```

Queries and events have the same pair — `IQueryPreInterceptor`, `IEventPreInterceptor` — and
they behave identically.

## Observing

The untyped form takes the base marker and returns it. Return the input unchanged when you are
only watching:

```csharp
public sealed class LogCommands(ILogger<LogCommands> logger) : ICommandPreInterceptor
{
    public ValueTask<object> HandleAsync(ICommand command, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        logger.LogInformation("Dispatching {Command}", command.GetType().Name);
        return ValueTask.FromResult<object>(command);
    }
}
```

## Rewriting

The typed form returns the typed message, so a rewrite needs no cast:

```csharp
public sealed class NormalizeName : ICommandPreInterceptor<CreateProduct>
{
    public ValueTask<CreateProduct> HandleAsync(CreateProduct command, ErgosfareContext context)
        => ValueTask.FromResult(command with { Name = command.Name.Trim() });
}
```

What the next stage receives is what you returned. Chain two of them and the second sees the
first's output.

Returning a **derived** message is allowed, and is often the point — `TCommand` is the contract,
and anything assignable to it satisfies it:

```csharp
public sealed class Enrich : ICommandPreInterceptor<PlaceOrder>
{
    public ValueTask<PlaceOrder> HandleAsync(PlaceOrder command, ErgosfareContext context)
        => ValueTask.FromResult<PlaceOrder>(new ValidatedPlaceOrder(command, _clock.UtcNow));
}
```

The handler still receives it as a `PlaceOrder`. A handler that wants the enriched shape should
declare the enriched type as its message instead.

{% aside type="note" %}
Earlier previews had `ICommandPreInterceptor<TCommand, TModified>` for exactly this. It was
removed once the one-parameter form started returning the typed message, because the second
argument then bought nothing. Migration is a deletion: drop the second type argument.
{% /aside %}

## Rejecting

To stop the dispatch, abort:

```csharp
public sealed class RequireTenant(ITenantAccessor tenant) : ICommandPreInterceptor
{
    public ValueTask<object> HandleAsync(ICommand command, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        if (tenant.Current is null)
            context.Abort("no tenant on this request");

        return ValueTask.FromResult<object>(command);
    }
}
```

`Abort` throws `ExecutionAbortedException`, which the pipeline treats as a stop rather than a
failure: **the exception stage does not run, and neither does the final stage.** The signal
travels to the caller unchanged.

Throwing your own exception instead is a different statement — that says the dispatch *failed*,
and it runs the failure path. Use whichever you actually mean.

{% aside type="caution" %}
Because an abort skips the final stage, anything a pre-interceptor opened — a transaction, a
lease — will not be closed there. Put the check ahead of whatever opens the resource with a
higher `[Weight]`, or open it somewhere a `try`/`finally` covers.
{% /aside %}

## Passing information forward

The message is not the only channel. When a later stage needs something that does not belong in
the message, use the context:

```csharp
public sealed class Correlate : ICommandPreInterceptor
{
    public ValueTask<object> HandleAsync(ICommand command, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        context.Set("correlationId", Guid.NewGuid());
        return ValueTask.FromResult<object>(command);
    }
}
```

## Ordering

Within the stage: `[Weight]` descending, direct registrations before covariant ones. Be explicit
whenever two interceptors genuinely depend on the order:

```csharp
[Weight(100)] public sealed class Authorize : ICommandPreInterceptor { … }
[Weight(50)]  public sealed class Validate  : ICommandPreInterceptor { … }
```

## Typical uses

**Validation.** Reject early, before the handler touches anything:

```csharp
public sealed class ValidateOrders : ICommandPreInterceptor<PlaceOrder>
{
    public ValueTask<PlaceOrder> HandleAsync(PlaceOrder command, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        if (command.Lines.Count == 0)
            context.Abort("an order needs at least one line");

        return ValueTask.FromResult(command);
    }
}
```

**Authorization**, at a high weight so it runs first.

**Normalisation** — trimming, casing, defaulting — so every handler downstream sees clean input.

**Correlation and timing**, paired with a final interceptor that reads what this one set.

## Scope it narrowly

An interceptor written against `ICommand` runs for every command in the application, including
nested dispatches. Narrow it to a base type you own when the work is not genuinely universal —
you get the typed message as well, which is worth having on its own.

This walks the whole surface once. Everything here compiles against the preview packages
described in [Installation](/ergosfare.docs/preview/getting-started/installation).

## 1. Register Ergosfare

One call at the composition root. `AddGenerated()` is written by the source generator and
names every construct it found:

```csharp
using Stella.Ergosfare.Core.Extensions.MicrosoftDependencyInjection;
using Stella.Ergosfare.Commands.Extensions.MicrosoftDependencyInjection;
using Stella.Ergosfare.Queries.Extensions.MicrosoftDependencyInjection;
using Stella.Ergosfare.Events.Extensions.MicrosoftDependencyInjection;

var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);

builder.Services.AddErgosfare(registry => registry
    .AddCommandModule(module => module.AddGenerated())
    .AddQueryModule(module => module.AddGenerated())
    .AddEventModule(module => module.AddGenerated()));
```

Register only the modules you use. A project with no events needs no event module.

## 2. A command

A command is an instruction. It has exactly one handler, and it either produces a result or it
does not:

```csharp
using Stella.Ergosfare.Commands.Abstractions;
using Stella.Ergosfare.Core.Abstractions;

public sealed record CreateProduct(string Name, decimal Price) : ICommand<Guid>;

public sealed class CreateProductHandler(IProductRepository repository)
    : ICommandHandler<CreateProduct, Guid>
{
    public async ValueTask<Guid> HandleAsync(CreateProduct command, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        var product = new Product(command.Name, command.Price);
        await repository.AddAsync(product, context.CancellationToken);
        return product.Id;
    }
}
```

The handler is an ordinary class. Its constructor parameters are resolved from the dispatching
scope, so a scoped `IProductRepository` behaves exactly as it does in a controller.

Dispatch it:

```csharp
app.MapPost("/products", async (CreateProduct command, ICommandMediator mediator) =>
{
    var id = await mediator.SendAsync(command);
    return Results.Created($"/products/{id}", id);
});
```

For a command with no result, implement `ICommand` and `ICommandHandler<TCommand>`, and the
handler returns a plain `ValueTask`.

## 3. A query

A query asks a question and changes nothing:

```csharp
using Stella.Ergosfare.Queries.Abstractions;

public sealed record GetProduct(Guid Id) : IQuery<ProductDto>;

public sealed class GetProductHandler(IProductRepository repository)
    : IQueryHandler<GetProduct, ProductDto>
{
    public async ValueTask<ProductDto> HandleAsync(GetProduct query, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        var product = await repository.FindAsync(query.Id, context.CancellationToken);
        return ProductDto.From(product);
    }
}
```

```csharp
var product = await mediator.QueryAsync(new GetProduct(id));
```

For a sequence, declare `IStreamQuery<T>` and implement `IStreamQueryHandler<TQuery, T>`. Its
handler returns `IAsyncEnumerable<T>` and the caller reads it with `await foreach`.

## 4. An event

An event is something that already happened. Every matching subscriber runs, and none of them
answers the publisher:

```csharp
using Stella.Ergosfare.Events.Abstractions;

public sealed record ProductCreated(Guid Id, string Name);

public sealed class SendWelcomeEmail(IEmailSender sender) : IEventHandler<ProductCreated>
{
    public ValueTask HandleAsync(ProductCreated @event, ErgosfareContext context)
        => sender.SendAsync($"Product {@event.Name} created", context.CancellationToken);
}
```

Notice that `ProductCreated` implements nothing. Writing the subscriber is what makes it an
event, so the project that declares your domain types needs no Ergosfare reference.

```csharp
await mediator.PublishAsync(new ProductCreated(id, name));
```

{% aside type="note" %}
A publish nobody subscribes to is a build error, not a silent no-op. If you write the event and
forget the subscriber, the compiler says so with `ERGO005` rather than letting the call return
having delivered nothing.
{% /aside %}

## 5. An interceptor

Interceptors are how cross-cutting work attaches without the handler knowing. This one runs
before every command:

```csharp
using Stella.Ergosfare.Core.Abstractions;

public sealed class LogCommands(ILogger<LogCommands> logger) : ICommandPreInterceptor
{
    public ValueTask<object> HandleAsync(ICommand command, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        logger.LogInformation("Dispatching {Command}", command.GetType().Name);
        return ValueTask.FromResult<object>(command);
    }
}
```

It needs no registration of its own — `AddGenerated()` already found it. Narrow it to one
message by implementing `ICommandPreInterceptor<CreateProduct>`, which also lets you return the
typed message:

```csharp
public sealed class TrimName : ICommandPreInterceptor<CreateProduct>
{
    public ValueTask<CreateProduct> HandleAsync(CreateProduct command, ErgosfareContext context)
        => ValueTask.FromResult(command with { Name = command.Name.Trim() });
}
```

There are four stages — pre, post, exception and final — covered in
[Interceptors](/ergosfare.docs/preview/core-concepts/interceptors).

## 6. Build it

Build, and read what the compiler tells you. Ergosfare reports what it can prove:

```
error ERGO005: This dispatch of 'ArchiveProduct' can never reach a handler…
warning ERGO007: Handler 'ReindexProduct' handles 'ReindexProduct', but no dispatch site in
the compiled closure can deliver that message — the handler never executes.
```

Neither is a lint suggestion. The first says a call will always fail; the second says code you
wrote will never run. See
[Working with diagnostics](/ergosfare.docs/preview/best-practices/diagnostics) for how to read
them, and when suppressing one is the right answer.

