  the generator can prove, and what it declines to guess.
- [Best practices](/ergosfare.docs/preview/best-practices/messages-and-handlers) — shaping
  messages so the compiler can help you.

A queue consumer reads bytes and has to turn them into a dispatch. The mechanics are
straightforward; the parts worth getting right are the DI scope, the acknowledgement boundary,
and keeping enough static type information that the compiler can still help you.

## The shape

```csharp
public sealed class OrderConsumer(
    IServiceScopeFactory scopeFactory,
    ILogger<OrderConsumer> logger) : BackgroundService
{
    protected override async Task ExecuteAsync(CancellationToken stoppingToken)
    {
        await foreach (var delivery in _channel.ReadAllAsync(stoppingToken))
        {
            using var scope = scopeFactory.CreateScope();
            var mediator = scope.ServiceProvider.GetRequiredService<ICommandMediator>();

            try
            {
                var command = JsonSerializer.Deserialize<PlaceOrder>(delivery.Body.Span)!;
                await mediator.SendAsync(command, stoppingToken);
                await _channel.BasicAckAsync(delivery.DeliveryTag, false, stoppingToken);
            }
            catch (Exception ex)
            {
                logger.LogError(ex, "delivery {Tag} failed", delivery.DeliveryTag);
                await _channel.BasicNackAsync(delivery.DeliveryTag, false, requeue: false, stoppingToken);
            }
        }
    }
}
```

## One scope per message

This is the part to get right. A `BackgroundService` is a singleton, so it cannot inject a
mediator directly — the handler's scoped dependencies would come from the root scope and live
forever.

```csharp
using var scope = scopeFactory.CreateScope();
var mediator = scope.ServiceProvider.GetRequiredService<ICommandMediator>();
```

One scope per delivery gives each message its own `DbContext`, its own unit of work, and
disposal when it finishes — the same lifetime an HTTP request would get.

## Keep the static type as specific as you can

A consumer that knows what it deserialized should dispatch through the specific mediator:

```csharp
var command = JsonSerializer.Deserialize<PlaceOrder>(delivery.Body.Span)!;
await mediator.SendAsync(command, stoppingToken);
```

That keeps the dispatch provable: the compiler sees `PlaceOrder`, so `ERGO005` still applies if
nothing handles it.

A consumer that dispatches whatever arrives loses that:

```csharp
IMessage message = Deserialize(delivery);
await messageMediator.DispatchAsync(message, stoppingToken);   // opaque
```

This works, and sometimes it is genuinely what you want — a replay tool, a dead-letter drain.
But the static type proves nothing about which message arrives, so reachability can only reason
in aggregate. Turn on `ERGO009` if you want those sites flagged:

```ini
dotnet_diagnostic.ERGO009.severity = warning
```

## Routing by message type

The usual shape is a routing key or a header naming the type. Keep the mapping explicit rather
than resolving a `Type` by name — a computed type cannot be dispatched provably, and if it
reaches a `Register` call it is `ERGO018`:

```csharp
private static readonly Dictionary<string, Func<ReadOnlyMemory<byte>, ICommand>> Routes = new()
{
    ["order.place"]  = body => JsonSerializer.Deserialize<PlaceOrder>(body.Span)!,
    ["order.cancel"] = body => JsonSerializer.Deserialize<CancelOrder>(body.Span)!,
};
```

```csharp
if (!Routes.TryGetValue(delivery.RoutingKey, out var deserialize))
{
    await _channel.BasicNackAsync(delivery.DeliveryTag, false, requeue: false, stoppingToken);
    continue;
}

await mediator.SendAsync(deserialize(delivery.Body), stoppingToken);
```

Each entry names its type in source, so every one of those messages is still visible to the
compiler.

## Acknowledge after the dispatch

Ack when the handler returned, nack when it threw. Acknowledging first turns any failure into
silent data loss.

Whether to requeue is a decision the recipe cannot make for you: requeueing a message that fails
deterministically is an infinite loop. A dead-letter exchange with `requeue: false` is the
common answer.

## Cross-cutting work belongs in interceptors

Resist putting retry, logging and validation in the consumer loop — they apply to every message
and they are what interceptors are for:

```csharp
[Group("queue")]
public sealed class LogQueueDispatches : ICommandFinalInterceptor
{
    public ValueTask HandleAsync(
        IMessage message, object? result, Exception? exception, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        _metrics.Record(message.GetType().Name, succeeded: exception is null);
        return ValueTask.CompletedTask;
    }
}
```

```csharp
await mediator.SendAsync(command, GroupSet.Of("queue"), stoppingToken);
```

The group keeps the queue's pipeline separate from the one an HTTP request gets, without
duplicating a handler.

## Memoized handlers, maybe

A consumer whose handlers are genuinely stateless and whose dependencies are singletons can skip
per-dispatch resolution:

```csharp
services.AddErgosfare(registry => registry
    .ForceMemoizedHandlers()
    .AddCommandModule(module => module.AddGenerated()));
```

{% aside type="caution" %}
Only if that is really true. It keeps resolved participants, so a handler depending on anything
scoped captures an instance that outlives the scope that produced it. Most consumers touch a
database, which makes this the wrong choice.
{% /aside %}

## Publishing what happened

If the handler announces its work with an event, publish **after** the state is committed —
otherwise a subscriber can act on something that later rolls back. See the
[Unit of work recipe](/ergosfare.docs/preview/recipes/unit-of-work).

## Shutting down

Pass `stoppingToken` into the dispatch so a shutdown cancels in-flight work rather than being
waited out. If a message must not be interrupted halfway, do not pass it — but then nack on
shutdown so the broker redelivers.

A handler saves changes; something has to commit them. Doing that in every handler is the work
an interceptor exists to remove — and doing it *correctly* means being careful about when the
events go out.

## The shape

One transaction per command, opened before the handler and committed after it. A query gets
none, because it changes nothing.

```csharp
public interface IUnitOfWork
{
    ValueTask<IAsyncDisposable> BeginAsync(CancellationToken ct);
    ValueTask CommitAsync(CancellationToken ct);
}
```

## Marking the commands that want one

Not every command needs a transaction. A marker interface makes the choice explicit and lets
the interceptor narrow to it:

```csharp
public interface ITransactionalCommand : ICommand;

public sealed record PlaceOrder(Guid CustomerId, IReadOnlyList<Line> Lines)
    : ITransactionalCommand;
```

## The interceptors

A pre-interceptor opens, a final interceptor closes. Two stages, because the final stage runs
whether the handler succeeded or threw:

```csharp
public sealed class BeginTransaction(IUnitOfWork unitOfWork)
    : ICommandPreInterceptor<ITransactionalCommand>
{
    public async ValueTask<ITransactionalCommand> HandleAsync(
        ITransactionalCommand command, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        var transaction = await unitOfWork.BeginAsync(context.CancellationToken);
        context.Set("uow.transaction", transaction);
        return command;
    }
}
```

```csharp
public sealed class CompleteTransaction(IUnitOfWork unitOfWork, IEventMediator events)
    : ICommandFinalInterceptor<ITransactionalCommand>
{
    public async ValueTask HandleAsync(
        ITransactionalCommand command, object? result, Exception? exception, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        if (!context.TryGet<IAsyncDisposable>("uow.transaction", out var transaction))
            return;

        await using (transaction)
        {
            if (exception is not null)
                return;                                   // dispose rolls back

            await unitOfWork.CommitAsync(context.CancellationToken);
        }

        await PublishPendingAsync(context);
    }
}
```

The final stage is the right place for the close because it runs on both paths. Disposing
without committing is the rollback, so there is no separate failure branch to keep in sync.

{% aside type="caution" %}
A final interceptor does **not** run after `context.Abort()`. An abort is a deliberate stop, not
a failure, and it skips the exception and final stages — so a transaction opened in the pre
stage would leak.

Either abort *before* opening one (put authorization ahead of `BeginTransaction` with a higher
`[Weight]`), or open the transaction in the handler where a `try`/`finally` covers every exit.
{% /aside %}

## Publishing events after the commit

This is the part that is easy to get wrong. Publishing inside the transaction lets a subscriber
act on state that later rolls back:

```csharp
// the email goes out for an order that never existed
await repository.SaveAsync(order, context.CancellationToken);
await events.PublishAsync(new OrderPlaced(order.Id), context);
await unitOfWork.CommitAsync(context.CancellationToken);
```

Collect them instead, and let the final interceptor publish once the commit has succeeded:

```csharp
public static class PendingEvents
{
    private const string Key = "uow.events";

    public static void Enqueue(this ErgosfareContext context, object @event)
    {
        if (!context.TryGet<List<object>>(Key, out var pending))
            context.Set(Key, pending = []);

        pending.Add(@event);
    }

    public static IReadOnlyList<object> Drain(this ErgosfareContext context)
        => context.TryGet<List<object>>(Key, out var pending) ? pending : [];
}
```

The handler announces without dispatching:

```csharp
public sealed class PlaceOrderHandler(IOrderRepository repository)
    : ICommandHandler<PlaceOrder, Guid>
{
    public async ValueTask<Guid> HandleAsync(PlaceOrder command, ErgosfareContext context)
    {
        var order = Order.Create(command.CustomerId, command.Lines);
        await repository.AddAsync(order, context.CancellationToken);

        context.Enqueue(new OrderPlaced(order.Id));
        return order.Id;
    }
}
```

And the final interceptor drains the queue after committing:

```csharp
private async ValueTask PublishPendingAsync(ErgosfareContext context)
{
    foreach (var @event in context.Drain())
        await events.PublishAsync(@event, context);
}
```

Passing `context` keeps the publish inside the same logical operation — same items, same
cancellation. See [Nested dispatch](/ergosfare.docs/preview/core-concepts/nested-dispatch).

## Registering it

Nothing extra. Both interceptors are discovered like any other participant:

```csharp
services.AddScoped<IUnitOfWork, EfUnitOfWork>();

services.AddErgosfare(registry => registry
    .AddCommandModule(module => module.AddGenerated())
    .AddEventModule(module => module.AddGenerated()));
```

Order the pre stage explicitly if anything else must run before the transaction opens:

```csharp
[Weight(100)] public sealed class Authorize         : ICommandPreInterceptor { … }
[Weight(50)]  public sealed class BeginTransaction  : ICommandPreInterceptor<ITransactionalCommand> { … }
```

## When to reach for an outbox instead

Publishing after the commit closes the "subscriber saw a rolled-back order" hole. It does not
close the one where the process dies between the commit and the publish — the order exists and
nobody was told.

If that matters, write the events into the same transaction as an outbox table and let a
separate reader publish them. The interceptor above becomes the thing that *writes* the outbox
rows rather than the thing that publishes, and the shape stays the same.

## What this does not do

**It does not nest.** An inner dispatch shares the scope, so it shares the transaction. Nothing
here opens a second one, and nothing commits early.

**It does not decide isolation.** That belongs to your `IUnitOfWork`.

**It does not apply to queries.** They carry no marker, so the interceptor never matches them.

